WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:11.000
This is the chapter's lab: choose an example, the update mode, the tie rule and the visiting order.

2
00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:17.533
Press Step to update one node. The panel on the right shows that vote in detail.

3
00:00:17.533 --> 00:00:24.200
Two more steps and it is C's turn: B = 1 + 4 = 5, D = 2, E = 1. The gold row wins.

4
00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:28.267
To the end: 2 rounds, status stable, cut 3.

5
00:00:28.267 --> 00:00:34.212
The log below lists every step; click any row to jump back to it.

6
00:00:34.212 --> 00:00:42.212
Switch to synchronous updates and jump to the end: the same graph oscillates, and the status is cycle.

7
00:00:42.212 --> 00:00:49.212
Back to asynchronous, then Reverse order: 3 rounds, other label names, the same groups.

8
00:00:49.212 --> 00:00:55.536
Raise the C–D bridge to 10: the groups become AB | CDE, cut 5.

9
00:00:55.536 --> 00:01:01.469
The code lab: your code runs in the browser, with no Python to install.

10
00:01:01.469 --> 00:01:07.936
Run the checks first: neither task is written yet, so the result is INCOMPLETE.

11
00:01:07.936 --> 00:01:18.138
Task 1, count_votes: add up the neighbours' edge weights per label.

12
00:01:18.138 --> 00:01:24.871
Run the checks again: the vote counting passes; the stopping test is still missing.

13
00:01:24.871 --> 00:01:36.968
Task 2: a round with no change is stable; a state seen before is a cycle.

14
00:01:36.968 --> 00:01:44.901
Run the checks: everything passes, including the original 24-cell circuit and 28-person contact list.

15
00:01:44.901 --> 00:01:53.435
Below, your own solve() runs and is compared with the course engine step by step. Pick two nodes, synchronous.

16
00:01:53.435 --> 00:02:00.267
Your code reports a cycle too, and agrees with the course engine at every step.

17
00:02:00.267 --> 00:02:07.400
Finally, try the challenges to check you really understand. Results stay in your browser.
